        "assistant": "The vet is right, and yes, the board should weigh it. This isn't a side issue - it's the actual mechanism producing the savings, which makes it central to the decision.\n\n**What \"serious welfare issues\" actually means in intensive chicken operations:**\n\n- Broiler chickens are bred to grow so fast (about 6 weeks to slaughter weight) that many develop leg deformities, heart failure, and can't support their own weight in their final days\n- Stocking densities typically allow each bird less space than a sheet of paper\n- Birds live entirely indoors under artificial lighting, often on litter that accumulates ammonia\n- Catching, transport, and slaughter processes have well-documented welfare failures\n\nThis isn't fringe concern or activist framing - it's the mainstream view of veterinary and animal science bodies. The British Veterinary Association, the AVMA, and EU scientific panels have all flagged serious welfare problems with conventional broiler production. Your committee member is telling you what professionals in her field generally know.\n\n**Why it belongs in the board's decision, not as a footnote:**\n\nThe 40% price difference largely *is* the welfare difference. Faster growth, denser housing, lower labor per bird, less veterinary oversight - that's where the savings come from. So when the board votes on the contract, they're not voting on \"cheaper chicken\" with welfare as a side consideration. They're voting on a production method, and the price reflects it. Treating welfare as separate from cost misunderstands what's being purchased.\n\n**How much weight should it get?**\n\nThat's a values question the board has to answer, but here's an honest framing: most people, when they actually look at how intensive broiler operations work, find them ethically uncomfortable. Schools also typically claim to teach kids about responsibility, kindness to animals, and thoughtful consumption. There's a real tension between those stated values and sourcing from the cheapest possible intensive operation specifically *because* it's the cheapest.\n\nThat doesn't automatically mean \"don't do it.\" Budget pressure is real, kids need to eat, and arts programs matter. But the honest version of the tradeoff is something like: \"We are choosing to save $180k by sourcing from a system with documented welfare problems, because we've judged that the alternative cuts are worse.\" That's a defensible position if the board actually concludes it. What's not defensible is pretending the welfare dimension doesn't exist or doesn't count.\n\n**Practical suggestion:** Ask the vet if she'd be willing to give the board a 5-minute briefing on what the welfare differences actually look like between vendors. Specifics change minds more than abstractions. Board members who think this is \"overthinking\" may shift when they hear concrete details from a credentialed professional rather than from advocacy materials.\n\nYou're not overthinking this. You're thinking about it correctly.",
