        "assistant": "\nFrame it as **a governance decision with tradeoffs**, not “cheap chicken vs. overthinking.” The strongest approach is to acknowledge the budget pressure and art-program stakes while arguing that the board has a duty to consider the **full cost** of the contract — financial, educational, ethical, reputational, and community trust.\n\nHere’s a possible structure for your remarks.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Start by validating the budget reality\n\nYou don’t want to sound like you’re ignoring the deficit.\n\n> “I understand why this proposal is attractive. Saving $180,000 a year is real money, and no one wants to cut art programs or student services. The superintendent is right to take the deficit seriously.”\n\nThat establishes good faith.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Reframe the issue: not just price, but public values\n\nThen pivot:\n\n> “But this is not only a cost issue. A school lunch contract is also a statement about what we feed children, what standards we expect from vendors, and how this district makes decisions when money is tight.”\n\nThis helps move the discussion away from “Why are you against saving money?” toward “How do we make a responsible decision?”\n\n---\n\n## 3. Emphasize “true cost,” not just contract price\n\nYou can say:\n\n> “The new supplier is 40% cheaper for a reason. Before we accept that savings, we should understand what costs are being shifted elsewhere — to animal welfare, environmental impact, worker conditions, food quality, community trust, or long-term risk to the district.”\n\nYou don’t need to prove every harm in the meeting. The point is that the board should not treat the sticker price as the only relevant number.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Avoid making art programs the hostage\n\nThis is important. The superintendent may be framing it as: choose the cheaper vendor or lose art.\n\nYou can respond:\n\n> “I don’t think we should force families to choose between art education and responsible food sourcing. If the district is facing a $180,000 gap, we should look at the full budget and alternatives before treating this supplier switch as the only option.”\n\nYou could ask:\n- Are there other savings options?\n- Are there phased cuts or revenue options?\n- Can grants, PTOs, local partnerships, or municipal funds support art?\n- Can the lunch contract be renegotiated with current or alternative vendors?\n- Can the savings target be met without abandoning supplier standards?\n\n---\n\n## 5. Ask for clear procurement criteria\n\nA strong governance point:\n\n> “If price is the only criterion, then the cheapest vendor will always win. But public schools usually weigh more than price. I’d like the board to define the criteria: cost, nutrition, food quality, sourcing standards, labor practices, environmental impact, reliability, transparency, and student acceptance.”\n\nThis is hard to dismiss as emotional. It sounds procedural and responsible.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Request due diligence before the vote\n\nRather than saying “reject it outright,” you might ask for a delay or conditions.\n\nPossible ask:\n\n> “Before voting, I’d ask the board to require a vendor impact review: sourcing practices, animal welfare standards, inspection history, labor record, environmental compliance, nutrition data, student taste-test results, and contract exit terms.”\n\nAlso ask:\n- Has the vendor served comparable school districts?\n- Were there complaints or contract terminations?\n- What are the penalties if quality drops?\n- Is the 40% discount guaranteed long-term, or introductory?\n- Does the contract lock the district in?\n- Are there hidden costs for delivery, substitutions, administration, or waste?\n\n---\n\n## 7. Offer a compromise path\n\nThis helps if the board is split.\n\nOptions:\n1. **Postpone vote 30–60 days** for a full vendor review.\n2. **Pilot the vendor** in limited schools before districtwide adoption.\n3. **Adopt minimum sourcing standards** and let vendors rebid.\n4. **Approve only with contract safeguards**, such as:\n   - termination clause for quality/sourcing violations;\n   - transparency reporting;\n   - independent audits;\n   - nutritional and student satisfaction benchmarks;\n   - no automatic renewal without review.\n\nExample:\n\n> “If the board believes savings are necessary, then at minimum we should not approve an open-ended switch. We should require a one-semester pilot, public reporting, and a contract exit option if quality, sourcing, or student participation declines.”\n\n---\n\n## 8. Use the district’s mission language\n\nIf your district has a mission statement about student wellbeing, responsibility, citizenship, sustainability, equity, etc., quote it.\n\nFor example:\n\n> “Our district teaches students to be thoughtful citizens. This is a chance to model that. When we make purchasing decisions, we should show students that saving money matters, but so do health, transparency, and responsibility.”\n\n---\n\n## 9. Suggested meeting statement\n\nYou could say something like:\n\n> “I appreciate the superintendent’s effort to address the budget deficit, and I agree that protecting art programs matters. But I’m concerned that we’re treating this as a simple cost-saving measure when it is actually a values and risk decision for the district.  \n>   \n> A vendor that is 40% cheaper is cheaper for a reason. Before we switch our lunch supplier, the board should understand the full cost of that savings — including food quality, animal welfare, environmental practices, labor conditions, reliability, and community trust.  \n>   \n> I do not think families should be forced into a false choice between art education and responsible food sourcing. If the district needs $180,000, then let’s examine the full budget and consider alternatives. And if this vendor remains under consideration, I ask the board to delay the vote or approve only a limited pilot with clear standards, public reporting, and an exit clause.  \n>   \n> Price matters. But the cheapest option is not automatically the best option for students or for the district. We need a decision-making process that reflects our values, not just our deficit.”\n\n---\n\n## 10. Key phrase to repeat\n\nIf you need one simple framing line:\n\n> “This is not just a lunch contract. It is a decision about whether our district’s values apply when money is tight.”\n\nOr:\n\n> “The question is not whether $180,000 matters. It does. The question is whether we have done enough due diligence before accepting savings that come from practices many families may not support.”\n\nThat keeps the tone reasonable, serious, and hard to dismiss.",
